Abstract
The use of molecules bridged between two electrodes as a stable rectifier is an important goal in molecular electronics. Until recently, however, and despite extensive experimental and theoretical work, many aspects of our fundamental understanding and practical challenges have remained unresolved and prevented the realization of such devices. Recent advances in custom-designed molecular systems with rectification ratios exceeding $10^{5}$ have now made these systems potentially competitive with existing silicon-based devices. Here, we provide an overview and critical analysis of recent progress in molecular rectification within single molecules, selfassembled monolayers, molecular multilayers, heterostructures, and metal–organic frameworks and coordination polymers. Examples of conceptually important and best-performing systems are discussed, alongside their rectification mechanisms. We present an outlook for the field, as well as prospects for the commercialization of molecular rectifiers.
## Review article
## Introduction
Complementary metal-oxide semiconductor (CMOS) elements in electronic devices have an intrinsic lower size limit of a few nanometres. This results from short-channel effects and a very thin oxide layer, which leads to tunnelling leakage current between individual electrodes. Moreover, the size of these components is also subject to the limitations of the lithography process. In this context, considerable effort has been expended over the past three decades to explore the feasibility of using molecules, with sizes of just a few nanometres, as an alternative to CMOS circuit elements. This has become a mainstream idea in materials science and nanotechnology, reflecting a growing interest in the use of molecular and organic materials in electronics and photovoltaics by both the scientific and industrial communities. The concept of molecular electronics is also found in nature, where essentially all electronic processes from photosynthesis to signal transmission are mediated by molecules and their assemblies (although electrical components, such as diodes and transistors, are not found in nature). An extension of conventional electronics to molecular building blocks would not only allow further miniaturization of electronic devices, but also provide a valuable opportunity to use the unique functionalities of molecules. Prototype systems are two-terminal molecular junctions, which are now frequently used to study the intrinsic properties of molecules at the nanometre scale1–3 and serve as a testbed for investigating the structure–function relationship in passive and stimuli-responsive charge transport4–6. This relationship is a key feature of molecular electronics, whereby the function of a molecular device can be controlled through the nature and electronic characteristics of the molecules (that is, through chemical synthesis)7–10.
Molecular electronics have been experimentally realized11–14 using different design frameworks and sophisticated fabrication and analysis techniques15–20. The whole family of basic electronic components, such as resistors, capacitors, diodes, switches, transistors (still a challenge) and memory elements, can be realized by embedding molecules between two conducting electrodes and contacting them — if necessary — by an additional electrode or an external stimulus21,22. Molecular diodes are among the most important of these components because of their active nature and usefulness in combination with other building blocks (such as switches) in electronic circuits. A molecular diode acts electronically, similar to its semiconductor counterpart, and is generally represented by a combination of p- and n-doped materials forming a p–n junction. This junction facilitates current flow in one direction and restricts the flow in the opposite direction, thus behaving as a rectifier. This concept has been adopted by Aviram and Ratner23, who suggested a combination of an electron-donating moiety (donor) and an electron-withdrawing moiety (acceptor) over an insulating σ-bridge as a molecular rectification device.
The key feature of all concepts of molecular rectification is asymmetry along the direction of charge transport in electrode/molecule/ electrode junctions24–27. This asymmetry relates to the intrinsic structure of the molecules (hole–electron transfer between asymmetrically placed frontier molecular orbitals: that is, the highest occupied molecular orbital (HOMO) or lowest unoccupied molecular orbital (LUMO)), specific placement of a molecule in the junction, asymmetric coupling of the molecule to the electrodes, and/or the nature of the electrode materials28,29. Asymmetric coupling of a molecule to the electrodes includes both electronic coupling, which is mediated by the anchoring groups, and electrostatic coupling, which defines how much an orbital of a molecule shifts in energy for a given bias30–33. This can be achieved using asymmetric molecules that feature either an asymmetric molecular backbone or different anchoring groups facing the electrodes24,34–37. Frequently, it is difficult to decide which factor (or combination of factors) mentioned is responsible for rectification. A common reason for an asymmetry in the junction is Schottky barriers at the electrode/molecule interfaces, which are primarily related to the work function mismatch and the presence of interfacial dipoles38. In addition to the intrinsic characteristics of the electrode materials and the molecules, the height of these interfacial barriers can affect rectification, which, in turn, is affected by partial oxidation and the surface morphology of the electrodes. Consequently, rectification has also been demonstrated for symmetric molecules39,40.
In general, the performance of a rectifier is primarily quantified by the rectification ratio $(R R)$ , which represents the ratio of the current density $(\boldsymbol{\jmath}$ at a given forward bias $(+V_{x})$ compared to the current density at the reverse bias of the same magnitude $(-V_{x})$ . Accordingly, $R R(V_{x})=|J(+V_{x})/J(-V_{x})|$ with the sign of the bias being usually chosen to yield $R R>1$ . For molecular rectifiers, the $R R$ depends strongly on the applied voltage. Thus, the bias value corresponding to a reported rectification ratio is an important parameter; additional parameters that should be reported alongside the RR and bias value are the switching frequency, endurance, retention and fidelity. The ultimate benchmark for the RR values is the lower range of CMOS devices $(R R\geq10^{5})$ , which we define as high for molecular rectifiers; we define reasonable and low values to be on the order of $10^{3}$ and $10^{-10^{2}}$ , respectively. The realization of molecular rectifiers with high RR values has turned out to be difficult and alternative concepts have been developed, which we discuss throughout this Review.
Despite the vast amount of experimental data collected with different platforms, it was not possible until very recently to achieve a rectification ratio with molecular rectifiers comparable to that of CMOS devices; moreover, the systems reported to date have suffered from limited stability and reproducibility of the molecular junctions and from operating voltages exceeding those of CMOS devices (typically 1 V)24,35,37,41,42. A representative overview of the relevant systems can be found elsewhere41, complemented by a focused description of single-molecule and large-area systems2,11. During the past five years, molecular rectifiers with reasonable and high rectification ratios have been reported43–47, paving the way towards commercial implementation of such devices. In this Review, we showcase these recent advances for different material platforms: single molecules, self-assembled monolayers (SAMs), molecular multilayers, heterostructures and metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) and coordination polymers (CPs). The emphasis is put on a critical analysis of the advantages and disadvantages of the above platforms and on the most representative and promising (in our opinion) systems. For brevity, we avoid a detailed description of the experimental setups, which can be found in other reviews2,6,11.
## Single-molecule-based rectifiers
Of the different platforms used for molecular rectification, singlemolecule rectifiers are arguably the most challenging. This is because they represent the minimum size limit and are intrinsically quantum mechanical devices, exhibiting electronic characteristics that probably cannot be achieved otherwise. In their CMOS counterparts, the main functionality originates from the electronic structure of the donor and acceptor parts. By contrast, the use of molecules allows experimentation with resonant versus off-resonant transport48,49, mechanical and electrical switching50,51, and spin as an information carrier52,53, in addition to traditional transistor and rectifier characteristics, as originally
## Review article
suggested by Aviram and Ratner23. Nevertheless, connecting a single molecule to an electrode in a device is not an easy task, and to do this in a controlled and reproducible fashion is even more difficult. However, advances in molecular manipulation techniques at the nanometre scale have made it possible to obtain controlled and reproducible results. Such techniques include mechanically controlled break junctions54–56 and scanning tunnelling microscopy (STM)57,58 for single-molecule junctions, and conductive-probe atomic force microscopy (CP-AFM)16,59–61 for molecular-layer studies. Studying the effect of gating on mechanically controlled break junctions and variation of the electrode material in CP-AFM-based junctions is particularly attractive for controlling and understanding rectification. With purpose-specific molecular design and improved instrumentation, effects beyond charge transport, including thermoelectricity, quantum interference and spin transport, have been addressed at the single-molecule level15,62–64. Complementary advances have also been made in first-principles calculation methods for studying rectification behaviour. Most notably, theoretical approaches based on Green’s function theory (that is, many-body theory) allow researchers to explore the intrinsic properties of single molecules under non-equilibrium conditions65,66.
The electron-transport properties of a single-molecule junction depend on the atomic-scale details of the molecule–electrode contacts. Consequently, there is a large discrepancy between the experimental and theoretical data, as well as a large variation among datasets from different laboratories. Single-molecule conductance is usually measured statistically because a single conductance trace does not yield reliable data. Accordingly, many conductance traces are usually recorded and analysed as conductance histograms from which the single-molecule conductance is determined. These measurements can be complemented by inelastic electron tunnelling spectroscopy and surface-enhanced Raman spectroscopy at the single-molecule length scale67,68. Such studies contribute greatly to our understanding of the chemical and structural behaviour of single molecules in the charge-transport process.
Although rectification in a single-molecule junction was first experimentally realized in 2005, a very low RR $_{(<10)}$ and uncertainty regarding the underlying charge-transport mechanisms34,69 had — until recently — cast doubt on the practical relevance of this concept. Significant progress has been made during the last decade, leading to singlemolecule rectifiers that rival their silicon-based counterparts, at least in terms of rectification performance. Most notably, Aragonès et al.70 achieved an RR exceeding $4\times10^{3}$ using an STM-break-junction approach under ambient conditions for 1,8-nonodiyne molecules deposited on low-doped silicon electrodes with high mechanical stability. These authors also stressed that the integration of single molecules with semiconductors provided compatibility with conventional semiconducting circuitry. In another example, Perrin et al. demonstrated a gate-tunable, single-molecule diode with an RR of $6\times10^{2}$ using an asymmetrically substituted dithiol molecule embedded into a mechanically controlled break junction setup47. These authors proposed a mechanism of rectification that was directly linked to the molecular structure of the asymmetrically substituted dithiol molecule and the presence of two conjugated states, which were weakly coupled through a saturated linker that broke the conjugation. The findings of this study demonstrate that specific electronic functionality can be implemented in single molecules by optimizing the internal molecular structure. In a more recent study using the CP-AFM method, Atesci et al.71 showed that the RR value of Ru-polypyridyl complexes assembled in a monomolecular manner on a transparent and conducting indium tin oxide (ITO) substrate could be changed by more than three orders of magnitude (between 1 and $10^{3^{\cdot}}$ ) by simply toggling the relative humidity between $5\%$ and $60\%$ (Fig. 1). The key to this behaviour is the presence of the two localized molecular orbitals in series, which are nearly degenerate in a dry state but become misaligned under high humidity owing to the displacement of counter ions $(\mathsf{P F}_{6}^{-})$ .
Today, the main challenges faced by single-molecule electronics (for rectifiers and other components) are the need to improve reproducibility and device stability. For a mechanically controlled break junction, if the electrode nanogap changes slightly, the system can adopt a different configuration that might be either stable or unstable. The main difference between the stable and unstable junctions can then be the difference in the structure of the immediate contact region between the molecule and electrodes, which can vary from junction to junction and heavily influence the reproducibility of the I–V traces. In general, the current detected in single-molecule junctions is very small (a few nanoamperes); it is therefore extremely sensitive to external vibrations, probe resistance, the shape of the electrodes, and the exact molecule–electrode contact geometry. In addition, for practical applications, molecular devices cannot be isolated from external environmental factors, such as temperature, humidity and electric field. These factors sensitively influence the electronic structure of the molecule and parameters of the junction. Furthermore, reproducibility is affected by molecular degradation, which is another factor that needs to be considered when designing molecular devices and performing the experiments. Moreover, the experimental realization of molecular rectification generally requires a bias voltage of $\mathsf{\Pi}_{\geq1}\mathsf{v}_{\|}$ at which the stability of a single-molecule junction can become a limiting factor for reliable performance. Another practical challenge is designing and fabricating (at commercial scale) single-molecule-junction platforms that can be integrated into existing CMOS circuits.
## SAM-based rectifiers
Two-dimensional (2D) molecular ensembles, such as self-assembled monolayers (SAMs), are more appealing than single molecules for practical devices owing to their simpler fabrication, higher stability, higher compatibility with large-scale production and lower cost72–75. Densely packed SAMs can be arranged on different substrates, such as metals, oxides and oxide-free silicon76. Of the various SAMs, monolayers with thiolate anchoring groups, originating mostly from thiols or dithiols, are especially popular for use in large-area molecular junctions77–83. The advantages of these systems include the high affinity of thiols or dithiols to coinage metals, their simple preparation (in most cases), reproducibility and versatility. The disadvantages include a longer self-assembly time than other systems, defect formation and high chemical reactivity, which frequently leads to partial oxidation of the SAM-forming molecules, diminishing their coupling to the substrate19,84.
Researchers can rationally design and fine-tune the rectification behaviour of a molecular junction by modifying the chemical structure of SAMs and/or by varying the characteristics of the electrodes. The mechanism of rectification depends on the molecular structure and specific experimental platform. In particular, the length of the molecular backbone can strongly affect the performance of a molecular junction because it influences the thickness (tunnelling barrier), conformation and conformational homogeneity of the SAM9. In early research using Langmuir–Blodgett films and SAMs, very low rectification values $\left(R<30\right)$ were reported. A breakthrough came in 2009, when Nijhuis et al. placed SAMs of alkanethiols with a terminal, redox-active ferrocene (Fc) moiety between a bottom Ag electrode and top eutectic
## Review article
GaIn electrode to achieve an RR value of about $10^{2}$ at $\pm1\upnu$ (refs. $^{85-87})$ . These authors explained the observed $R R$ value as arising from the ferrocene HOMO (single-molecule level) being positioned asymmetrically in the junctions, thus allowing for resonant tunnelling across the ferrocene moiety at a sufficiently high negative bias86,87.
In a subsequent study, a SAM composed of $\S(\mathrm{CH}_{2})_{11}$ (4-MeBIPY) (where 4-MeBIPY is 4-methyl-2,2′-bipyrid-4′-yl)88 displayed an RR value of $85\pm2$ at $\pm1.0\mathsf{V}.$ This value is comparable with that of the ferrocenebased monolayers but with opposite polarity (that is, higher current at positive bias), which is mainly because of the nature of the SAM. In more recent work, SAMs of ${\mathsf{S}}({\mathsf{C H}}_{2})_{11}{\mathsf{B I P Y}}$ (where BIPY is bipyridine) complexed with a metal ion (cobalt or copper) were integrated into molecular junctions with Au and eutectic GaIn electrodes89. In this example, the junctions featuring the BIPY– $\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{CoCl}_{2}$ moiety showed an RR value of about 82 at $\pm1.0{\upnu}$ owing to the constructive involvement of the dominant molecular orbital (for example, the LUMO) at positive bias only. By contrast, rectification was not observed for the BIPY$\mathsf{C u C l}_{2}$ moieties because of the symmetrical involvement of the frontier molecular orbitals (HOMO or LUMO) at both positive and negative bias.
Using a combination of two ferrocene moieties instead of one (that is, biferrocene (Fc–Fc) or biferrocenylene $({\mathsf{F c}}{=}{\mathsf{F c}})$ ), Nijhuis and coworkers achieved an $R R$ of $10^{3}$ (ref. 43). The rectification in this case relied on sequential tunnelling across the junction, with resonant tunnelling over both HOMO and HOMO-1 levels of the Fc–Fc/F $:\mathsf{c}=\mathsf{F}($ moiety positioned asymmetrically within the junction. Although this mechanism is promising for yielding high RR values, it has not been systematically studied so far. Research in this area has extended to controlling the direction of rectification in a molecular diode by the placement of the ferrocene moieties within the molecular backbone77 and analysing the factors that affect the device performance77,79.
Following a similar experimental approach but using fullerene $\mathbf{(C_{60})}$ instead of ferrocene, Qiu et al. applied undecanethiolate SAMs with a fullerene tail group in a junction with a bottom Ag and top eutectic GaIn electrode to generate an RR value of up to 940 at $\pm\mathbf{1}\upnu$ (ref. 90). However, this value depended on the fullerene density, the composition of the top contact (eutectic GaIn) and the energy of the lowest unoccupied π-state of the SAM-forming molecules (Fig. 2a,b). It was higher than the RR value of a single-ferrocene-based junction under the same conditions (see above). The authors ascribed the aforementioned dependency to the smaller energy difference between the electrode Fermi level and LUMO of the fullerene (about $4.5\mathrm{eV})$ ; the larger surface area and spherical symmetry of fullerene molecules; and asymmetric metal (contact)–fullerene interactions. The junctions also show hysteresis in the current–voltage $(I{-}V)$ curve upon applying positive
