bias above $+0.45\mathrm{V}$ , which further increases at $+1.0$ V. The authors did not suggest a reason for the hysteresis, but we speculate that it may be caused by the ambient moisture affecting the coupling of the molecules to the top electrode.
Extending the two-level approach further, Nijhuis and co-workers44 used ${\sf S C}_{15}{\sf F c-C\equiv C}.$ −Fc molecules with low-lying HOMO and HOMO-1 levels located at the Fc− $\mathsf{C}\equiv\mathsf{C}$ −Fc units. This resulted in an RR value exceeding $10^{5}$ , although a high bias of $\pm3.0{\mathrm{V}}$ was required, which was only possible for a Pt substrate. The RR values were highly reproducible and comparable to the lower range found for CMOS-based devices $(R\geq10^{5})$ . As in the abovementioned examples from this group, the main mechanism of rectification was resonant tunnelling across the $\mathsf{F c-C}{\equiv}{\bf C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{F c}$ moiety. This was assisted by an increase in the number of conducting molecules at the negative polarity of the bias (compared to the positive polarity), driven by electrostatic interactions between the top electrode and charged terminal Fc groups.
The possibility of combining different mechanisms that operate outside the single-level tunnelling regime for high RR values has been recently explored. For example, a molecular system was reported in which a resistive switch and a current rectifier were combined to provide an unprecedented dual functionality of diode and a variable resistor. The system featured a SAMs of $\mathsf{S}(\mathsf{C H}_{2})_{11}\mathsf{M V}^{2+}\mathsf{X}_{2}^{-}$ , where $\ensuremath{\mathsf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathsf{V}}^{2+}$ is a dicationic methyl viologen, and $\mathsf{X}^{-}$ is the counter ion embedded between the Ag and eutectic GaIn electrodes45. This system is depicted in Fig. 2c,d. The RR was $2.5\times10^{4}$ (which is still high but lower than that of the previous example), and the resistive ON/OFF ratio was $6.7\times10^{3}$ . The mechanism of rectification in this system was attributed to dimerization of the $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{V}}^{2+}$ redox units accompanied by the directional migration of $\mathsf{X}^{-}$ ions under a negative bias. Another example of a system exhibiting properties of both a switch and a rectifier comprises monolayers of ferrocene- or ruthenocenyl-substituted biphenylthiolates and fluorenethiolates on Au(111) (ref. 91). These films showed an effective $R R$ of more than $10^{3}$ at a bias of just $\pm0.1\mathrm{v}$ (‘effective’ refers to the specific nature of bias variation; see ref. 92). The switching and rectification mechanism is not yet clear, but the involvement of an irreversible redox process is likely91,93.
As alternatives to the single-component SAMs discussed above, mixed SAMs have also been used for rectification, with particular emphasis placed on interstitially mixed monolayers. In these films, small molecules fill the interstices between the bulky molecules via repeated molecular exchange, minimizing the density of defects within the monolayer. Such specifically designed films can enhance the stability and reproducibility of molecular junctions without compromising their function and reliability. For example, Kong et al. fabricated interstitially mixed SAMs using $n$ -undecanethiol terminated with $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -bipyridyl $\mathrm{(HSC_{\mathrm{11}}B I P Y)}$ as an active matrix and $n$ -octanethiol as a reinforcement material94 with a continuous cyclic exchange of molecules in the SAMs to achieve the highest breakdown voltage. The experimental results combined with theoretical simulations confirmed a defect-free monolayer structure, yielding high operation stability and a noticeably broader voltage range $(\pm3.3\mathsf{V})$ than the corresponding traditional SAMs88. The interstitially mixed SAMs enabled direction reversal of rectification at higher bias than for traditional SAMs through the formation of more ordered, rigid and closely packed molecular layers.
Other potential candidates for molecular rectification are SAMforming biomolecules, such as oligopeptides, proteins and DNA92,95. A particular advantage of DNA is the possibility of fine-tuning its charge-transport properties by subtle alteration of its conformation via the ionic environment (that is, by changing the pH), methylation or intercalation with metal ions and complexes96–98. For example, in a representative study, a DNA-based molecular rectifier was constructed by site-specific intercalation of a charged small molecule (coralyne) into an 11-base-pair DNA complex, providing an RR value of about 15 at $\pm1.1\mathsf{V}$ in phosphate-buffered saline99. In another example, an oligopeptide-based diode was formed with platinum electrodes and peptide chains composed of two pairs of amino acids (alanine–lysine
## Review article
and threonine–alanine)100. The length of the peptide chains and the arrangement of the amino acids strongly influenced the rectification performance of the device.
A major obstacle for the commercial application of molecular junctions is the absence of reliable, manufacture-scalable device platforms that reproduce the unique and intrinsic functional properties of their component molecules. A promising solution is the electrochemical deposition of molecules onto electrode surfaces. For example, the McCreery group demonstrated that electrochemically grafted monolayers and multilayers on conducting carbon and other surfaces have good compatibility with vapour-deposited electrodes and conventional lithographic techniques. Their systems exhibited highly reproducible $(99.4\%$ , representing 718 of 722 tested devices) electrical properties in the laboratory as well as in commercially available products, such as guitar pedals, in which they improved the sound quality3.
## Multilayer molecular rectifiers
Multilayer structures in nature (such as butterfly wings and bird feathers) have inspired a range of artificial multilayer-based coatings and devices with exceptional performance in fields such as energy materials, metamaterials, optoelectronics, power and flexible electronics, and sensing and actuating systems101. Multilayer-based molecular rectifiers have also received significant attention because of their high internal quantum efficiency, high RR value and transconductance, and the feasibility of junction fabrication. Molecular bilayers based on the basic donor–acceptor concept are the most prevalent. In general, under forward bias, electrons flow from the acceptor to the donor side (Aviram– Ratner model23), provided the coupling between the donor and acceptor is stronger than the coupling between either layer and its respective electrode, which should still be sufficient for acquiring high currents and for rectification102–104. By contrast, if the coupling between the donor and acceptor layers is weaker than that between either layer and its respective electrode, electrons flow from donor to acceptor under forward bias (anti-Aviram–Ratner model)105,106. This donor–acceptor concept was tested in bilayers of $\mathbf{C}_{60}$ and pentacene films deposited on Cu(111), which were contacted from the top by an STM tip107. The resulting assembly was an Aviram–Ratner-type rectifier with RR values close to $10^{3}$ , albeit with lower RR values near the edges of the bilayers because of the confinement effect and the tip-induced Stark effect.
The extent of rectification depends on the molecular structure of the bilayer, as well as the nature of the contacts, internal dipoles, charge polarization, the presence of trapped molecules, and the parameters of the bilayer preparation system (including the solvent used, temperature and operating voltage)43,108. For example, Lopes et al. reported a temperature-regulated inversion of rectification direction in molecular diodes comprising a copper phthalocyanine (CuPc) and hexadecafluorophthalocyanine $(\mathsf{C u P c F}_{16})$ bilayer109. An RR value of $10^{2}$ , with electrons flowing from the $\mathrm{\left(CuPcF_{16}\right)}$ ) acceptor to the (CuPc) donor in the forward (larger current) direction, was observed at temperatures below $50\mathsf{K}$ owing to charge accumulation at the CuPcF $_{16}/\mathrm{CuPc}$ interface. However, the difference between $I(-V)$ and $I(+V)$ decreased with increasing temperature, with parity reached at 220 K. At room temperature, electrons preferentially flow from the donor to the acceptor, thus demonstrating temperature-driven inversion of rectification direction. This phenomenon was explained on the basis of the trap-mediated Poole–Frenkel mechanism. Other Aviram–Ratner-type rectifiers featuring donor– acceptor layers with large HOMO–LUMO gaps were reported to feature a constant rectification ratio of approximately 300 over a temperature range of 7–100 K (ref. 110).
In addition to the RR value, the frequency range of molecular rectifiers is important. This can be significantly extended by careful interface engineering using organic bilayer heterostructures. For example, junctions comprising an ultrathin ${\mathsf{C o P c F}}_{16}/{\mathsf{C u P c}}$ bilayer $(1\mathsf{n m}/7\mathsf{n m})$ exhibited rectification at frequencies up to $10\mathsf{M H z}$ (ref. 111). These devices showed an RR of about 400, which was ascribed to the different interfacial properties of the top and bottom Au electrodes. The high-frequency response was attributed to charge accumulation at the ${\mathsf{C o P c F}}_{16}/{\mathsf{C u P c}}$ interface and the good crystallinity of the CuPc layer.
The effect of light and solvents on the rectification behaviour of bilayer and multilayer molecular junctions has also been studied112. For example, light-induced switching of the rectification direction was observed for molecular junctions featuring Fe(II) bis-terpyridineappended dithienylethene (Fe-tpy-DTE-tpy)n photochromic films of variable thickness (Fig. 3a), which were fabricated by the layer-bylayer technique113. The junctions displayed an RR value of 200 in both photostationary states and an ON/OFF ratio of 120. The interaction of solvents with molecular rectifiers has also been investigated. For example, fluorene (FL) and benzoic acid (BA) were used to form a bilayer, in which $\boldsymbol{\mathsf{H}}^{+}$ ions of BA were replaced by Li+ ions forming lithium benzoate (LiBA) in a carbon $\mathrm{\langleFL_{4.5nm}/L i B A_{4.0n m}/}$ carbon stacking configuration112 (Fig. 3b). The conductance of the junctions containing mobile ions decreased sharply in the presence of a polar solvent $\mathsf{\Gamma}(\mathsf{C H}_{3}\mathsf{C N})$ owing to electric-field-induced solvent and ion reorganization. Although the RR value of these junctions was initially very low (1.3 at $\pm\bf{1}\nabla\psi_{\mathrm{{+}}}$ ), it increased to 18 after repetitive I–V scanning, and the junctions could be reset at $_{0\vee}$ .
In another study, McCreery et al. fabricated single and bilayer molecular junctions featuring anthraquinone (AQ), bis-thienylbenzene (BTB), and AQ-BTB combinations (Fig. 3c). The polarity of photoconduction and the rectification direction of the resulting devices depended strongly on the electronic structure of the molecular layers114. This group also synthesized a multifunctional trilayer memory device by stacking AQ, BTB and LiBA layers, and integrating this assembly into a junction with carbon electrodes as the bottom and top contacts (Fig. 3d). The RR value increased from 28 for bilayers to 75 for trilayers, and the trilayers exhibited scan-rate-dependent conductance switching in acetonitrile vapour115. Dynamic control of RR in molecular bilayers has also been reported for bilayers of triethyleneglycol-functionalized fulleropyrrolidines (PTEG-1)116. In that study, a stochastic 8-bit computation without the need for checksums was performed in a microfluidic device using eutectic GaIn as the top contact under a constant bias. The bitstream was written and read from the same group of molecular junctions, which removes the need to transmit the bits from memory to the processing unit (that is, the ‘von Neumann bottleneck’). We note that in stochastic computing, numbers are represented by the ratio of 1 and 0 bits rather than exact sequences of bits, which makes this method more tolerant to bit-flips (that is, errors). The state of the molecular layer was switched from rectifying bilayers to non-rectifying PTEG-1 using a string of droplets of toluene with and without PTEG-1 and bubbles of air, corresponding to an ON/OFF ratio of about 200.
Multilayer molecular rectifiers can also be used for brain-inspired memory devices, which display exceptional computing performance, tunability and adaptability117–119. In the most noteworthy example120, a combination of a dioctylbenzothienobenzothiophene ( $\mathrm{\Delta}[\mathrm{C}_{8}]$ -BTBT) bilayer with a modulated Schottky barrier diode was used to fabricate phototunable synaptic-like devices for neuromorphic computation to overcome the von Neumann bottleneck. The device featured
