The examples selected in this section highlight the various advantages of multilayer-based molecular rectifiers, which make them promising candidates for advanced technological applications.
## Review article
However, multilayer-based junctions also have serious drawbacks, such as complex and expensive fabrication processes and complex energy-band arrangements. These issues should be overcome or at least diminished for future commercialization of multilayer-based molecular rectifiers.
## Heterostructure-based rectifiers
Non-functionalized molecules, including conjugating ones, that provide symmetric tunnelling charge transport would not intuitively seem to be suitable molecular diodes121–123. Nevertheless, these molecules can demonstrate desired, asymmetric I–V characteristics in heterojunction structures because their interfacial band alignment can be altered via combination with other moieties. Design concepts for the fabrication of hybrid molecular junctions or heterojunctions have been developed, in which inorganic materials, such as nanoparticles and 2D semiconductors, are combined with molecular films124–126. For example, ZnO nanoparticles were electrostatically assembled over organic molecular films such as the xanthene stain rose bengal and the dye copper phthalocyanine to fabricate hybrid molecular junctions (n-type Si/n-ZnO, p-ZnO/organic molecules/Hg, n-type Si/organic molecules/n-ZnO or $\scriptstyle\mathbf{p}\cdot Z\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}/\mathbf{H}\mathbf{g})^{127}$ . Because of the donor–acceptor character of the nanoparticles and organic molecules, respectively, charge only flows in one direction, resulting in current rectification. In another study, metal-phthalocyanines and transition metal dichalcogenides $(\mathsf{M o S}_{2})$ were used as organic and inorganic materials to fabricate a heterojunction (via layer-by-layer electrostatic adsorption) featuring current rectification128 (Fig. 4a). The strength of the magnetization vector depended on the character of the 3d band of the central metal atom of the phthalocyanine. Accordingly, these molecules were aligned planarly on the electrode surface under an applied external magnetic field. This alignment was locked in place using a layer of cationic polyallylamine before removing the external magnetic field. The alignment altered the molecular orbitals and promoted better charge transfer between the electrode and phthalocyanine, which affected the energy levels at the interface and thus the RR values. In another representative study, $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{W S e}_{2}$ layers were combined with thiolate SAMs on Au as organic–inorganic heterojunctions129 (Fig. 4b). For SAMs alone, no asymmetry was observed $(R\approx1)$ ; however, the I–V curves changed markedly upon attachment of $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ and ${\mathsf{W S e}}_{2}$ becoming asymmetric and yielding RR values of $1.79\times10^{3}$ and 2.3 at $\pm\mathbf{1}\upnu$ for the biphenyl4-monothiol (OPT2) $\prime{\bf M o S}_{2}$ and OPT2/ $\mathrm{\mathrm{'}W S e}_{2}.$ junctions (where OPT2 is biphenyl-4-thiol), respectively.
## MOF- and CP-based rectifiers
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) and coordination polymers (CPs) are typically crystalline nanoporous materials formed by coordinating metal ions and organic linkers130,131. Initially, research on MOF and CPs focused on modulating their physical and chemical structure for applications such as gas storage, catalysis and separations132,133. In the past decade, MOFs and CPs have been integrated in solid-state electronic devices owing to their tunable electronic and optoelectronic properties134–138. However, these materials have barely been explored in molecular electronics because of the insulating nature of the organic linkers and poor overlap between the π-orbitals of these linkers and the $d$ -orbitals of the metal centres, resulting in electrical conductivity of $10^{-10}\mathsf{S c m}^{-1}$ or even lower. However, the conductivity can be improved by coupling the metal sites to the redox-active linkers, doping the MOFs with redox-active guest molecules103,139–141, or by introducing noncovalent interactions within coordination complexes (for example, H-bonding, $\uppi\mathrm{-}\uppi$ interactions, van der Waals forces and anion–π interactions)10,102,142,143.
The abovementioned efforts to increase the conductivity have paved the way for the use of MOFs and CPs in molecular electronic devices144. In 2017, the first efficient, CP-based molecular diodes were developed using Cd(II) coordination polymers, such as [Cd(4-bpd) $(\mathsf{S C N})_{2}]_{n}$ and $\operatorname{\{Cd}}_{4}\mathbf{L}_{2}(\mathsf{N C O})_{6}\right]_{n}$ complexes, where 4-bpd is 1,4-bis(4- pyridyl)-2,3-diazo-1,3-butadiene and L is 2-methoxy-6-((quinolin8-ylimino)methyl)phenol145,146 (Fig. 5a). Upon exposure to light, these devices displayed distinct rectification behaviour, which could be optimized by structural variation of the CP linker moieties. Until recently,
