Another factor affecting the electrical conductivity of CPs and MOFs is the introduction of anionic species. In a representative study, ${\mathsf{B}}{\mathsf{F}}_{4}^{\mathrm{~-~}}$ ionic species were used as guests in a three-dimensional network of $Z\mathsf{n}(\mathsf{I I})$ MOFs with neutral bis-pyrazole-based ligands148. In these systems, the porous channels formed inside the MOFs were populated with H-bonded anions and anion-solvent clusters, which enhanced the electrical conductivity. When integrated into a two-terminal junction with a bottom ITO and a top Al electrode, current rectification was observed, albeit with a low RR value of 15–19 (Fig. 5b).
In another study, electronically conducting MOFs (EC-MOFs) were used as an effective interlayer material to modulate the Schottky barrier in self-powered Schottky diodes, which is essential for photodetector applications. Moreover, a series of highly oriented triphenylene-based EC-MOF thin films, ${\bf M}_{3}({\bf C}_{18}{\sf H}_{6}{\sf X}_{6})_{2}$ (where M is Ni or Cu and X is O or NH) was used to optimize the silicon-based Ag/EC-MOF/n-Si Schottky junctions149 (Fig. 5c). The Schottky barrier was tuned from 0.67 eV in Ag/n-Si to 1.11 eV in Ag/EC-MOF/n-Si junctions by varying the EC-MOF components. However, preparing such high-quality EC-MOF thin films is rather challenging.
## Review article
Recently, the basic elements of crystalline organic semiconductors, such as anthracene and fullerene, have also been applied as linker moieties to fabricate surface-anchored MOFs (SURMOFs)150. Using a layer-by-layer approach, crystalline $\mathbf{Cu}_{2}(\mathbf{adc})_{2}$ (dabco) (where adc is 9,10-anthracene dicarboxylate) and $\mathbf{C}_{60}@\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}_{2}(\mathbf{bdc})_{2}(\mathbf{dabco})_{\boldsymbol{\imath}}$ (where bdc is 1,4-benzene dicarboxylate and dabco is 1,4-diazabicyclo[2.2.2] octane) layers, denoted p-SURMOF and n-SURMOF, respectively, were successively grown on a pre-functionalized gold electrode forming a p-SURMOF/n-SURMOF assembly (Fig. 6a,b). The MOFs of different conductivity were thus combined over a crystalline heterointerface, building the molecular devices exhibiting a diode-like behaviour with an RR value of approximately six orders of magnitude. Figure 6c shows the energy profile diagram, where the purple and green lines represent the HOMO and LUMO of the p/n-SURMOFs. Here, anthracene and fullerene are the active moieties, mediating the charge transport across the assembly via a charge-hopping mechanism, which is quite common in organic semiconductors at room temperature. In general, the p–n junction configurations obtained for the exemplary SURMOFs by either selective doping147 or successive growth150 can be extended to other MOF systems, enabling the fabrication of p–n-like heterojunctions with exceptionally high RR values. This highlights the potential of MOFs and CPs for molecular rectification.
Despite the progress achieved so far, large-scale production of reliable MOF-based devices is still difficult. In particular, it is challenging to prepare high-quality and well ordered crystalline SURMOFs. A better understanding of the growth process and the development of new fabrication approaches are needed. In addition, it is necessary to expand the available ligand designs and coordination chemistry. We nevertheless believe that the scalable formation of conducting MOFs via controlled preparation routes and their facile fabrication on conducting substrates will be possible in the near future, making MOF-based molecular electronics a reality.
## Comparison of different systems
Having introduced the different concepts of molecular rectification with representative examples, we now provide a brief comparison of the performance of these systems. We benchmark their performance using the RR and the respective bias, comparing these values among the systems discussed and with conventional CMOS-based devices (Table 1). The RR values for the best-performing SAM-, multilayer-, and CP- or MOF-based devices are sufficiently high to compete with CMOS-based devices. For example, $R R$ values of $6.3\times10^{5}$ and about $10^{6}$ have been achieved for SAMs of Fc− $\mathbf{C}\equiv\mathbf{C}$ −Fc-decorated alkanethiols on Pt (ref. 44) and $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{adc})_{2}(\mathrm{dabco})/\mathrm{C}_{60}@\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{bdc})_{2}^{\prime}$ (dabco) SURMOF assembly151, respectively. Thus, the value of RR is no longer the critical issue for molecular rectifiers, although it remains desirable to increase it further. The main technological challenges to realizing practical applications and commercialization of molecular rectifiers are: the development of reproducible fabrication routes that can be adapted to large-scale production; integration with other elements of an electronic circuit; and achieving reproducible performance and temporal stability of the molecular junctions, including stability against bias cycling. It is also important to develop devices that operate at practical voltages and frequencies. In most cases the operating voltage is comparable with that of CMOSbased devices (Table 1); however, the frequency range has remained largely unexplored for most molecular rectifiers. We note, however, that the smaller dielectric constants of organic molecules compared to inorganic materials, such as Si, and the fast charge transport in molecular junctions can — in principle — reduce the capacitance of the device and enable high-frequency operation in compact, high-density circuits152.
## Review article
The practically accessible size of molecular rectifiers is another important issue. The need for miniaturization of functional elements of electronic circuits was the initial (and remains a main) driving force for the development of molecular electronics. The ultimate miniaturization is provided by single-molecule devices. However, these devices feature RR values of the order of $10^{3}$ at the most, which is much lower than both CMOS-based analogues and other classes of molecular rectifiers. Moreover, these devices have inherently low reproducibility owing to the difficulty in precise control of molecular orientation at the interfaces, as well as limited integration capability with CMOS-based elements. The more effective (at least in terms of $R$ ; Table 1) SAM-, multilayer-, and CP- or MOF-based rectifiers feature molecular assemblies with a nanoscale vertical size but a larger lateral size, defined by the contact area of the electrodes in the laboratory experiments. Further studies of such systems should detail how far the lateral size of the rectifiers can be reduced without compromising their performance.
The most promising laboratory setups and fabrication routes for large-scale production include electrochemical deposition of molecules onto the electrode surface153, lithographic fabrication of large-area rectifying junctions154, mask-free optically induced electrodeposition and patterning of the contacts155, and photopolymerizationinduced encapsulation of untethered liquid top contacts156. Emphasis should be put not only on reproducibility, but also on the fidelity and endurance of the fabricated devices and related electronic circuits.
There are also various other important issues relating to device fabrication. For example, although the fabrication of three-terminal devices is routine in break-junction cases, it is much more challenging in large-area vertical, two-terminal molecular junctions. Using graphene as one of the contacts on top of the gate electrode in a vertical three-electrode system can enable gating of the energy levels of the chromophores close to the graphene contact and control of the rectification using a gate voltage157. A related issue is coupling of molecules at the interfaces, with a variety of bonding motifs and specific interactions, affected both by the characteristics of the molecules and the character of the electrodes. The use of more-stable carbene bonding to gold electrodes than the less-stable and oxidation-prone thiolate anchors151 can increase the reliability, reproducibility and stability of the molecular devices. Better control is also needed over the roughness of the bottom electrode, which is predominantly achieved using template-striped metal supports.
Finally, the rational design of molecular rectification requires a much deeper understanding than we currently have of the relationship between the molecular structure and charge transport behaviour
