in specific two-terminal junctions. This will require significant joint experimental and theoretical efforts.
## Proposed mechanisms of rectification
As mentioned in the previous section, understanding the mechanisms that govern rectification in molecular junctions is crucial for optimizing the properties of these systems2,158,159. An important factor for rectification is the asymmetry of the junction. Even when the same contact chemistry (that is, the nature of the bonding between molecule and contact) for the top and bottom contacts is used, the geometries of these contacts are usually different. This results in a different coupling strength at the two contacts, which can cause rectification in the sequential tunnelling regime160. This situation is described by the Datta–Paulsson model of rectification151. According to this model, for a symmetric molecule with LUMO-based conduction, the asymmetry in coupling strength to the two contacts forces the electrons to spend a longer time in the molecule when leaving for the contact with smaller coupling strength, and this leads to charging of the molecule, raising the energy level of the LUMO161. Therefore, in principle, a larger current is expected when the strongly coupled contact is biased positive for the electron to leave quickly. In addition, in sequential tunnelling, different interfacial tunnelling barrier capacitances can cause rectification162. By contrast, when the mechanism of charge transport is single-step tunnelling from one contact to the other, rectification is not expected for a symmetric molecule with the same contacts.
In molecular junctions, it is more common to have two different contact chemistries35 or an asymmetric placement of the main transport molecular level between the contacts77. In these cases, the apparent asymmetry cannot be solely ascribed to differing coupling strengths at the two contacts, especially when using metallic electrodes28. Instead, the different contact resistances at the electrodes lead to an asymmetric voltage drop40. Although an assumption of linear voltage drop inside the molecular junction (or an abrupt and equal V/2 voltage drop on each contact) might be experimentally justified in certain situations163, it is often not correct. An extreme case of unequal voltage drop features a sharp tip as one contact and a planar surface as the second contact in a solution containing ions. The application of a bias then results in excessive surface charge at the sharp tip, which draws more ions with the opposite polarity and causes the formation of a compact double layer and a large voltage drop at the tip164. Even the injection of a carrier into a local molecular level close to the metallic contact can modify the local electric field and result in a voltage drop. This induces an image charge in the contact area, which, together with the local carriers, acts like a double layer165.
The formation of two different interfacial dipoles at the two contacts or Fermi level pinning at only one contact can also result in asymmetric voltage drop and rectification25,166–168. However, strong rectification is not expected when the orientation of the dipoles is sufficiently disordered to broaden the interfacial states into resonance with the contact Fermi level169 and single-step tunnelling from these interfacial states to the opposite contact is the main charge-transport mechanism in the molecular layer. Although static disorder can eliminate rectification because of its destructive effect on the alignment of the surface dipoles9, dynamic and voltage-dependent disorder may cause rectification in some cases44,170.
The Kornilovitch–Bratkovsky–Williams model describes mole­ cular rectification due to asymmetric tunnelling barriers and therefore an asymmetric voltage drop171. In this model, positioning the resonant conducting molecular level closer to one contact than to the middle of the junction causes asymmetric coupling of this level to the two contacts through the respective tunnelling barriers. There is a greater voltage drop on one side of the conducting level than on the other side of the junction, assuming that the insulating materials on both sides of the conducting level have the same polarizability. Hence, it would be easier to bring the contact that is spatially further away from the conducting level into resonance with this level. However, a smaller voltage drop on the side of the shorter insulator means that the conducting level follows the position of the Fermi level on the short side closely and does not come into resonance with it easily. Alternatively, the use of two different contact materials with differing work functions can also affect (or cause) rectification behaviour. In the strong-coupling regime, this occurs through stronger charge transfer (dipole formation and voltage drop) on one side of the molecular layer; in the weak-coupling regime, this is due to the formation of a built-in electric field172, which can result in asymmetric polarization in the molecular layer.
Finally, we discuss the most common approach to fabrication of molecular rectifiers — the use of molecules with the HOMO localized closer to one contact and the LUMO localized closer to the other contact. The most well known concept in this context is the ‘electron donor–bridge–electron acceptor’ (D–B–A) proposed by Aviram and Ratner23. In this concept, a bridge represents a tunnelling barrier between the donor and the acceptor. The bridge is formed by an alkane or is simply a σ-bridge between the two π moieties with a torsion angle24. The Aviram–Ratner mechanism features multistep incoherent tunnelling, in which larger current flows in the forward direction when the contact closer to the donor moiety is biased positive and the contact closer to the acceptor moiety is biased negative. This results in hole tunnelling from the positive contact to the HOMO of the donor, and electron tunnelling from the negative contact to the LUMO of the acceptor. In the reverse direction, it is more difficult to bring the HOMO of the acceptor or the LUMO of the donor into resonance with the contacts, resulting in a delay of the onset of current flow173. Therefore, the direction of rectification is the same as that in semiconductor p–n junctions, in which the p- and n-type semiconductors are hole and electron conductors, respectively.
Many molecular junctions with a D–B–A structure follow an antiAviram–Ratner mechanism in which the forward direction corresponds to electron flow from the donor side to the acceptor side174. This behaviour is equivalent to current flow from n- to p-type semiconductor in a p–n junction and is much more likely to be inelastic in nature175. However, even a coherent tunnelling mechanism can, in principle, give rise to rectification in a multilayer barrier structure with strong coupling (ref. 176). The anti-Aviram–Ratner mechanism arises in molecular junctions featuring strong coupling between the contacts and the donor and acceptor moieties. One theoretically predicted approach to create a molecular rectifier with an anti-Aviram–Ratner mechanism is to pin the HOMO to one contact and the LUMO to the other contact using HOMO- and LUMO-aligning anchoring groups39. In another theoretical study, it was proposed that the barrier height and length of the bridge between the donor and acceptor, the HOMO of the donor, and the LUMO of the acceptor may be pinned when they reach resonance, owing to fast charge recombination177.
Pinning the acceptor’s LUMO to one contact and the donor’s HOMO to the other contact, separated by an alkane tunnelling barrier, has been experimentally shown to result in rectification via the anti-Aviram–Ratner mechanism178. The pinning would mean an almost constant HOMO offset for the donor and LUMO offset for the acceptor, regardless of the applied voltage, and would prevent the LUMO of the
