The Marcus inverted regime occurs when the HOMO of the donor and LUMO of the acceptor are crossed in energy and the $\mathsf{H O M O^{+}}$ (that is, when the HOMO level loses an electron and becomes positively charged) and the LUMO− (that is, when the LUMO level acquires an electron and becomes negatively charged) are stabilized by a sufficiently high voltage from the adjacent contact to reduce the activation energy to zero179. However, the LUMO− needs to be more strongly coupled to the positively biased contact than the LUMO, which may be because of electrostatic interactions. In the inverted regime, the electrons no longer need to acquire thermal energy to go from the donor to the acceptor, but lose some thermal energy upon arriving at the acceptor. The hole (left behind at the donor) and the electron (left behind at the acceptor) tunnel to their respective adjacent contacts. This mechanism maintains rectification even at low temperatures, down to 118 K. It was also reported that the voltage required to enter the Marcus inverted regime depends on the polarizability of the medium in which the D–B–A is embedded. Lower polarizability — achieved by diluting the
D–B–A layer with alkanes — results in a higher required bias to enter the inverted regime because it is more difficult to stabilize the charge in the acceptor and donor in a medium with lower dielectric constant180. This probably means that it is more difficult to enter the Marcus inverted regime in single-molecule devices.
Another mechanism of rectification involves the coupling of two magnetic molecules with single spins. A low conductance regime can then arise through a spin-singlet blockade, even in junctions without magnetic contacts181. However, achieving molecular rectification through spin interactions experimentally will require careful spininterface engineering182. In addition, attaching molecules with stable spins to D–B–A structures may help in gaining insight into the degree of coupling between the donor and acceptor132 and in studying the coherence via photoexcitation of the D–B–A structure183.
Frequently, it is difficult to distinguish between the mechanisms behind rectification in a particular experiment. For example, a strict Aviram–Ratner diode is difficult to realize practically because complete decoupling of the donor and acceptor energy levels is not always guaranteed184. Moreover, charge-transport experiments often suffer from uncontrolled coupling between the terminal groups of the molecules and the electrodes because of the character of a terminal group-electrode interaction that is not entirely controlled and because of random contact geometries185. Therefore, a D–B–A-based rectifier may also have an asymmetric voltage drop at the contacts owing to the formation of dipoles.
We also note that the basic theoretical models described above do not exclude more complex models that reflect the specific features and parameters of particular molecular systems186, such as the involvement of several energy levels, redox processes, quantum
