interference, changes of molecular conformation or the involvement of a solvent91,93,176,187–190. Such models, along with quantum mechanical simulations, are frequently derived in the context of specific experiments to rationalize the results. As our understanding of the chargetransport mechanisms in molecular junctions evolves, new design concepts for molecular rectifiers are likely to be proposed, and the current models may be revisited and modified accordingly.
## Outlook
Silicon-based electronic components are soon likely to reach their minimum size limit. Considering the level of control exercised at present in industrial foundries, it is difficult to predict whether molecular-based electronic circuitry will replace silicon-based components in standard devices. Nevertheless, we are hopeful that this circuitry will at least find specific applications and fill specific market niches. In general, a competitor for existing CMOS technologies should not only meet the specifications described in the International Technology Roadmap for Semiconductors, but should also be superior to the existing technologies in at least one key area, such as power consumption, fabrication cost, and/or performance (for example, reliability, durability, response time, complexity of electronic functions and so on). In the context of these requirements, molecular electronics has a long way to go before it can be a mature, competitive technology.
The key steps to advancing molecular electronics to a competitive level are: a deeper understanding of the relationship between the molecular structure and charge-transport properties, which is necessary for the rational design of molecular rectifiers; better control of the molecule–electrode interfaces; developing technologies for large-scale manufacturing of molecular electronic devices; and ensuring their robust performance and stability. This will require significant experimental and technological efforts, as well as progress in theory, calling for commitment from both the scientific community and industry. In addition, the issues of thermal dissipation and heat generation inside the molecular diodes and their effect on device longevity should be carefully investigated, especially at high currents and voltages, which are frequently needed for efficient rectification. Changes in environmental parameters, such as humidity, should also be considered, which may involve the use of protective packaging of molecular diodes to achieve reliable performance. Although electrodeposition of molecules is promising for the fabrication of high-stability, largearea molecular diodes, standard SAM fabrication techniques are more suitable for smaller-area and faster diodes, albeit with lower thermal stability. MOF-based molecular junctions may offer higher stability and the ability to specifically self-assemble on electrode surfaces, although their three-dimensional and often redox-active nature may give rise to unwanted charge trapping and device ageing.
In summary, it is at present difficult to say which approach to molecular rectification will first reach the requirements for practical, commercial application. However, the progress achieved so far allows us to hope that molecular rectifiers will become a useful and important part of future microelectronic technology.
